{"type":"standard","title":"Tsuutʼina language","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tsuutʼina language</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q20825","titles":{"canonical":"Tsuutʼina_language","normalized":"Tsuutʼina language","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Tsuutʼina language</span></span>"},"pageid":2490816,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Tsuut%CA%BCina_map.svg/330px-Tsuut%CA%BCina_map.svg.png","width":330,"height":276},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Tsuut%CA%BCina_map.svg/960px-Tsuut%CA%BCina_map.svg.png","width":512,"height":428},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1351069607","tid":"42090388-40de-11f1-9d6e-f20fcb2c8121","timestamp":"2026-04-25T19:37:56Z","description":"Athabaskan language of Alberta","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tsuut%CA%BCina_language"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuut%CA%BCina_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Tsuut%CA%BCina_language"}},"extract":"Tsuutʼina, formerly known as Sarcee or Sarsi, is an Athabaskan language spoken by the people of the Tsuutʼina Nation, whose reserve and community is near Calgary, Alberta. It is related to other Athabaskan languages, such as Navajo and Chiricahua to the south, and Dene Suline and Tłı̨chǫ to the north.","extract_html":"<p><b>Tsuutʼina</b>, formerly known as <b>Sarcee</b> or <b>Sarsi</b>, is an Athabaskan language spoken by the people of the Tsuutʼina Nation, whose reserve and community is near Calgary, Alberta. It is related to other Athabaskan languages, such as Navajo and Chiricahua to the south, and Dene Suline and Tłı̨chǫ to the north.</p>"}